+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` already provides `resolvePointer` (RFC 6901), which throws `JsonPointerError`. It rejects `-` and cannot return a parent, so patching needs its own token parsing.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+- Goals: follow RFC 6902 exactly; no input mutation; no dependencies.
+- Non-goals: JSON Merge Patch, patch generation (diff), options such as in-place application.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Export a `parsePointer(pointer): string[]` helper from the pointer code (refactoring `resolvePointer` to use it) so escape and syntax rules live in one place.
+- Deep-clone the document up front (`structuredClone`), and clone values taken from the patch on `add`/`replace`/`copy`/`move`, so the result shares nothing with inputs. Operations then mutate the working clone; since failure throws, no partial result escapes.
+- Own-property handling: use `Object.hasOwn` for existence checks and `Object.defineProperty` for setting members so keys like `__proto__` are plain data and cannot pollute prototypes.
+- Array indices must match `^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$`; `-` is allowed only for `add` targets.
+- `move` checks the proper-prefix rule on token arrays, no-ops when `from` equals `path`, otherwise removes then adds. 
+- `test` uses a recursive JSON equality function (order-insensitive objects).
+- Errors: a `JsonPatchError` class (like `JsonPointerError`) for invalid patches and failed operations; pointer syntax errors may propagate as `JsonPointerError`.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- Removing the root throws. Unknown extra members on an operation are ignored (RFC 6902 §4). Duplicate-member/non-JSON inputs (undefined, functions) are out of scope.
+
+## Risks
+
+- `structuredClone` accepts non-JSON values; input is assumed to be JSON.
